From 16730138190e1aeacffad895bdd89b492b583134 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xs1128 <ooixinsheng@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 10:33:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 23/24] RHS: one slab for the state; fused kernels take the
 slabs as explicit-shape arrays

The fused WENO5+HLLC kernel loaded each variable's stencil through that
variable's Fortran descriptor (q_prim_vf(i)%sf), and its flux stores went
through module allocatables, so after every store the compiler reloaded
their descriptors. Per-line SASS counts showed 16 % of the kernel's
instructions in the stencil address arithmetic alone.

The conservative fields (and the saved RK stage) now live in one 4-D array
each, q_slab and q_stor_slab, and q_cons_qp%vf(j)%sf are pointer views of
their layers. q_slab also holds the primitive fields that are not aliases
of the conservative ones, in layers sys_size + 1 : 2*sys_size; qp_layer(i)
is the layer of primitive i. The fused kernel reads its stencil from
q_slab and writes flux_rsx_vf, flux_src_rsx_vf and vel_src_rsx_vf, all
passed down as explicit-shape dummies, so their bounds are fixed at entry:
on the x sweep the six stencil loads become one address plus immediate
offsets, and stores no longer force reloads. The fused RK update takes the
slabs the same way when the stage state is q_cons_qp (rk_slabs).

Same operations in the same order: results are bitwise identical.
perf3d, 1 GPU (H200), same node per A/B: 33.13 -> 30.07 ms per step.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
